WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.300
Welcome to Grade 8 English, Unit 8:
Global Storytelling and Creativity.

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.400
Welcome to Unit 8 of your Year 8 English course.

3
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.200
This unit is called Global Storytelling and Creativity,

4
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.780
and it is one of the most exciting
units you will explore this year.

5
00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:21.600
Throughout this unit, you will discover
how stories shape the world around us,

6
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:28.580
how creative people use language to inspire and persuade,
and how you can develop your own storytelling skills.

7
00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.860
You will read a powerful original
story called The Edge of the Infinite,

8
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:41.600
which follows a young Somali storyteller named Hannah as
she competes in a national youth storytelling competition in

9
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.740
Dar es Salaam,

10
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.120
Tanzania.

11
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:50.540
Her journey will show you that great stories do not simply
entertain people; they change lives, preserve memories,

12
00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.020
and give voice to communities
that might otherwise be forgotten.

13
00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.060
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

14
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.060
By the end of this unit,

15
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:08.560
you will be able to summarise the central ideas and
important details in global storytelling and creativity texts.

16
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:13.140
you will also support inferences and
comparisons with relevant text evidence.

17
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.100
you will also use grade 8 vocabulary
accurately in discussion and writing.

18
00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.350
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

19
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:29.140
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
crusade, clarify, assail, infinite, motivate, stamina.

20
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.940
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

21
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.600
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

22
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:47.240
The reading sequence includes Global Storytelling and
Creativity: source text 1 and Global Storytelling and Creativity:

23
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.120
source text 2.

24
00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:50.600
Read actively.

25
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:55.340
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

26
00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.980
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

27
00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.480
The first language focus is How to Form It.

28
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:12.140
The structure is: Subject + has/have + been + verb-ing
Use have been with I, you, we, they: I have been studying.

29
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:18.400
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

30
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:23.560
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Story Retelling.

31
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.220
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

32
00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.800
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

33
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.580
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

34
00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.720
You are ready to begin.

35
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:45.580
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

36
00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:48.420
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

37
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:54.760
Use AI English for hints and feedback, and
bring your questions to the live sessions.
